a_and_Artificial_Intelligence", "publisher": "Wikipedia"}]}-->
<p>For years, AI and data policy in the UAE was spread across several bodies: an AI office within the federal government, the digital government function inside the telecoms regulator, a data office, and emirate-level and free zone authorities alongside them. Teams working here learned to ask "which regulator?" before "which rule?". That is starting to change.</p>
<p>This is an architect's reading of public announcements and law firm commentary, not legal advice.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What was announced</h2>
<p>In June 2026 the UAE approved the establishment of the Federal Authority for Artificial Intelligence and Data, reporting to the Cabinet, as a unified national body for data governance, artificial intelligence and digital government<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Commentary reports that it brings together three existing functions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>:</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Previous body</th><th>What it covered</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Artificial Intelligence Office</td><td>National AI strategy and programmes</td></tr>
<tr><td>TDRA Information and Digital Government Sector</td><td>Federal digital government services and standards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Emirates Data Office</td><td>National data governance</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The mandate described in commentary covers the national AI strategy, government data governance, and standards and compliance across federal entities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. By October, reporting on the UAE's AI programme was already framing the authority as the body bringing national data, AI and digital government systems together<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>This week: Dubai's AI campus targets</h2>
<p>On 26 October, during the Dubai AI Festival, Dubai approved new targets for its AI campus: more than 1,000 AI companies and over 5,000 specialised jobs by 2030<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Together with federal consolidation, the direction is clear: one national voice on AI and data standards, and emirate-level competition to host the companies building on them.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Consolidation usually means fewer, clearer standards, applied more consistently. Teams that have been relying on ambiguity should plan for less of it.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What we don't know yet</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Private sector reach.</strong> The described mandate centres on federal entities. How far its standards will extend to private companies, directly or through procurement, is not yet clear.</li>
<li><strong>Interaction with free zones.</strong> DIFC and ADGM have their own data protection regimes, including <a href="/insights/difc-regulation-10-ai-systems/">DIFC Regulation 10 on AI systems</a>. How federal AI standards will sit alongside them remains to be seen.</li>
<li><strong>Data protection supervision.</strong> The federal <a href="/insights/uae-pdpl-ai-architecture-2026/">PDPL</a> continues to apply. Which body leads on its AI-related aspects will become clearer through guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Health data.</strong> Sector rules, such as those in <a href="/insights/uae-health-data-law-localisation/">the UAE health data law</a>, remain in place.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The regional pattern</h2>
<p>Saudi Arabia went down this path earlier, with the Saudi Data and Artificial Intelligence Authority as a single body for data and AI<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>, alongside its own data protection law covered in <a href="/insights/saudi-pdpl-cross-border-ai/">Saudi PDPL and cross-border AI</a>. A combined data and AI authority tends to treat data governance and AI governance as one discipline. Architecturally, that's right: most AI risk is data risk.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I'd prepare</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Map your current obligations</strong> by entity and location: federal PDPL, free zone regimes, sector rules, and where your models and data actually run, as discussed in <a href="/insights/uae-ai-infrastructure-where-to-run-models/">UAE AI infrastructure</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Build governance on a recognised framework</strong>, such as <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 or the NIST AI RMF</a>, so national standards become a mapping exercise.</li>
<li><strong>If you sell to government</strong>, expect federal data and AI standards to appear in procurement first. Keep an AI system inventory and data lineage ready to evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Unify data and AI governance internally.</strong> If the regulator treats them as one discipline, a separate AI committee disconnected from data owners will struggle.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for guidance</strong> from the new authority and plan a quarterly review of your mapping.</li>
</ol>
</section>

